.coverage
htmlcov/

# Project data and outputs (see IMPLEMENTATION.md section 2).
# Leading slash anchors these to the repo root -- an unanchored "data/" also
# matches src/dekf_bench/data/ and silently drops the package from commits.
/data/
/results/
# The default figure output directory (utils/paths.py). Generated: a PNG in the
# history goes stale the moment a run is re-tuned.
/figures/

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Meeting deliverables: the .docx summary and .pptx deck builders. They write to
# a personal OneDrive folder and produce supervisor-facing documents, so they
# help nobody run or check the benchmark. Kept on disk, out of the repo.
#
# A GLOB, because excluding these by name leaks. A builder written after the list
# was last edited sits untracked *and* unignored -- one `git add -A` from
# publication. That happened twice on the Mackey-Glass branch: once to
# make_mg_status_docx.py, patched by adding its name, and then to
# make_mg_presentation.py, which sat in that state for a day and stayed private
# only because every push in the window happened to stage by explicit path. The
# same weakness is here; nothing has fallen into it yet.
#
# The convention this rests on is stated further down: `report_*.py` is kept
# because a reader checking a number wants it, while `make_*` builds a deliverable
# and `run_*` runs an experiment. `plot_*.py` is already globbed on exactly that
# reasoning; the make_* list was the odd one out. The individual names are kept
# below because their comments record why each is out, not because the glob needs
# them.
/scripts/make_*.py
/scripts/make_presentation.py
/scripts/make_summary_docx.py
/scripts/make_drift_presentation.py
/scripts/make_drift_docx.py
/scripts/make_diffusion_presentation.py

# The figure layer, excluded on the same grounds. Every one of these draws a PNG
# for those documents: they answer "what should the slide look like", not "does
# the benchmark run" or "is the result right". A reader who wants to check a
# number wants `scripts/report_*.py`, which is kept, and the runs in `results/`.
#
# `tests/test_figures.py` goes with them rather than being orphaned -- it imports
# `make_figures` to check that figure axis labels match the data, so it tests a
# file that is no longer here and would fail to collect on a fresh clone.
/scripts/make_figures.py
/scripts/make_preliminary_figures.py
/scripts/plot_*.py
/tests/test_figures.py
